---
description: Rebuild only the working server-connected product, never dead old code
alwaysApply: true
---

# The working server-connected product is the source of truth

The old app was edited by many AI systems over time and contains dead files,
abandoned components, duplicate implementations and disconnected code. The old
repository is **not** the specification. The currently working, server-connected
product is.

Never reproduce an old module in the new app just because the file exists.

## Prove a thing is live before rebuilding it

Before rebuilding any screen, component, hook, service, store, API wrapper,
socket handler, asset or dependency, classify it:

- **ACTIVE** — used by the working app
- **ACTIVE INDIRECTLY** — reached via dynamic import, lazy route, event
  registration, WebSocket, deep link, push routing, native bridge or feature flag
- **DUPLICATE** — the same job is already done elsewhere
- **LEGACY** — superseded by the current implementation
- **DEAD** — no runtime path reaches it
- **UNKNOWN** — usage not yet proven

Rebuild ACTIVE and ACTIVE INDIRECTLY. Exclude DEAD, LEGACY and DUPLICATE.
Never rebuild UNKNOWN — resolve it first.

## DEAD requires evidence, not an unused-symbol warning

An editor's "unused" hint is not proof. Before calling anything DEAD, rule out
static imports, dynamic imports, lazy routes, route registration, navigation,
event listeners, WebSocket registration, background tasks, push routing, deep
links, native bridges, Capacitor plugins, Android/iOS references, build scripts
and feature flags.

State the evidence for the classification.

## Server endpoints are classified too

Map the backend against the client and label every route and socket event:
USED BY APP, USED BY ADMIN/OPS, USED BY EXTERNAL SERVICE, REQUIRED BUT CLIENT
MISSING, LEGACY/UNUSED, or UNKNOWN.

If the server has a real active product capability whose client side is missing,
build that client side. Do not expose admin or server-only functionality as user
UI. Do not invent a client feature because an abandoned API wrapper exists.

## Pick one implementation, not all of them

Where several old versions do the same job — multiple auth services, API clients,
socket clients, upload services, gift or chat handlers — find which one the
production path actually uses and rebuild that one cleanly.

## Parity means the same working product

Parity is judged on behaviour, not file count. If 1,000 old files contain 600
files' worth of live behaviour, the new app implements that behaviour cleanly. A
smaller new codebase that does everything the product does is correct.

The new project must contain no dead files, dead imports, abandoned routes,
unused services or stores, duplicate clients, placeholder logic or leftover
experiments.
